CALC1:010130.canonicalSolution:
At $x=0$, both radicands equal $1$. Because $1+\tan x$ and $1-\sin x$ are continuous at zero, each remains positive on some open interval about zero. Thus the displayed expression is real on a deleted two-sided neighborhood of zero.

Multiply by the conjugate. Distributing the subtraction across the second radicand gives
\[
\frac{\sqrt{1+\tan x}-\sqrt{1-\sin x}}{x}
=\frac{(1+\tan x)-(1-\sin x)}
{x\bigl(\sqrt{1+\tan x}+\sqrt{1-\sin x}\bigr)}
=\frac{\tan x+\sin x}
{x\bigl(\sqrt{1+\tan x}+\sqrt{1-\sin x}\bigr)}.
\]
Hence
\[
\frac{\tan x+\sin x}{x}
=\frac{\tan x}{x}+\frac{\sin x}{x}\longrightarrow 1+1=2,
\]
while the conjugate denominator tends to $1+1=2$. Therefore
\[
\boxed{\lim_{x\to0}
\frac{\sqrt{1+\tan x}-\sqrt{1-\sin x}}{x}=1}.
\]
The plus sign in the rationalized numerator is essential: subtracting $1-\sin x$ changes the sign of its sine term.

