CALC2:010325.canonicalSolution:
All terms are nonnegative, so Tonelli's theorem allows the order change:
$$
\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{H_n}{2^n}
=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\sum_{k=1}^n\frac{1}{k2^n}
=\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{1}{k}\sum_{n=k}^\infty2^{-n}.
$$
The inner geometric sum is $2^{1-k}$, hence
$$
\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{H_n}{2^n}
=2\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{(1/2)^k}{k}
=\boxed{2\ln2}.
$$
For the tail, use $H_n=H_N+\sum_{k=N+1}^n1/k$:
$$
R_N=H_N\sum_{n=N+1}^\infty2^{-n}
+\sum_{k=N+1}^\infty\frac{1}{k}\sum_{n=k}^\infty2^{-n}
=\frac{H_N}{2^N}+2\sum_{k=N+1}^\infty\frac{1}{k2^k}.
$$
This is the requested exact decomposition. Its second term is positive, giving the strict lower bound. Since $1/k\leq1/(N+1)$ for $k>N$,
$$
2\sum_{k=N+1}^\infty\frac{1}{k2^k}
\leq\frac{2}{N+1}\sum_{k=N+1}^\infty2^{-k}
=\frac{2^{1-N}}{N+1}.
$$
Combining these statements proves both bounds.

DS:010217.statement:
A circular buffer of fixed capacity $C=5$ is a triple $(\mathit{buf},h,n)$, where $\mathit{buf}[0..4]$ is a five-slot array, $h$ is an integer index, and $n$ is an integer size. The representation invariant is
\begin{align*}
(\mathrm{I}1)&\quad 0\le n\le 5,\\
(\mathrm{I}2)&\quad h\in\{0,1,2,3,4\},\\
(\mathrm{I}3)&\quad\text{the abstract sequence is }\bigl(\mathit{buf}[(h+i)\bmod 5]\bigr)_{i=0}^{n-1}.
\end{align*}
Slots whose indices are not among $\{(h+i)\bmod 5:0\le i<n\}$ may hold stale keys and are not part of the abstraction. The empty buffer is $(h,n)=(0,0)$ with every slot unmarked. Legal operations are
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{ENQ}(x)&:\text{require }n<5,\text{ write }x\text{ at }(h+n)\bmod 5,\text{ then }n\leftarrow n+1,\\
\mathrm{DEQ}()&:\text{require }n>0,\text{ read }\mathit{buf}[h],\text{ then }h\leftarrow(h+1)\bmod 5\text{ and }n\leftarrow n-1.
\end{align*}

1. Prove that every state satisfying (I1)--(I3) is empty if and only if $n=0$ and full if and only if $n=5$. Show that the next write index $(h+n)\bmod 5$ equals $h$ in both the empty case and the full case, so the predicate $h=(h+n)\bmod 5$ cannot distinguish them.

2. Starting from a valid state, prove that both operations re-establish (I1)--(I3) and realize the abstract sequence operations “append $x$” and “drop the first key.” Identify the exact occupied-index set before and after a wraparound write.

3. Starting empty, execute the operation sequence
   \[
   \mathrm{ENQ}(A),\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(B),\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(C),\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(D),\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(E),\;
   \mathrm{DEQ},\;
   \mathrm{DEQ},\;
   \mathrm{DEQ},\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(F),\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(G),\;
   \mathrm{ENQ}(H).
   \]
   Record $h$, $n$, the physical five-tuple, and the logical sequence after the five enqueues, after the three dequeues, and after the final three enqueues. Prove that the terminal physical array is $[F,G,H,D,E]$ and that the terminal logical sequence is $D,E,F,G,H$.

4. Audit both sentences: (i) “the physical array is the abstract sequence, so the terminal order is $F,G,H,D,E$”; (ii) “the next write index equals $h$, so the buffer is empty and $n$ may be ignored.”

Do not replace the ring buffer by a geometrically resizing dynamic table, and do not treat the structure as a sentinel-based deque splice.

DLOG:010631.statement:
Four HDL fragments are proposed. All identifiers are one-bit registers or wires as indicated by the implied hardware. Classify each fragment, repair the broken ones, and simulate the swap.

Fragment W, intended as a combinational mux:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\texttt{always @(a or sel) begin}\\
&\quad\texttt{if (sel) y = a; else y = b;}\\
&\texttt{end}
\end{aligned}
\]

Fragment X, intended to swap two flip-flops on each rising clock:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\texttt{always @(posedge clk) begin}\\
&\quad\texttt{a = b;}\\
&\quad\texttt{b = a;}\\
&\texttt{end}
\end{aligned}
\]

Fragment Y, intended as a loadable register:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\texttt{always @(posedge clk) begin}\\
&\quad\texttt{if (en) q <= d;}\\
&\texttt{end}
\end{aligned}
\]

Fragment Z, intended as a combinational mux:
\[
\begin{aligned}
&\texttt{always @(*) begin}\\
&\quad\texttt{if (sel) y = a;}\\
&\texttt{end}
\end{aligned}
\]

1. For each fragment name the inferred hardware: combinational logic, level-sensitive latch, edge-triggered flip-flop, or enabled flip-flop. Identify any simulation/synthesis mismatch.

2. Simulate fragment X for two rising edges from the initial state $(a,b)=(1,0)$. Then rewrite X with nonblocking assignments so that the two flip-flops swap, and give the two-edge trace of the rewrite from the same initial state.

3. Rewrite W so that simulation and synthesis both see a mux, and rewrite Z so that no latch is inferred. State the latch-inference rule that distinguishes Z from Y.

4. Audit these claims: (i) fragment Y is a latch because $q$ is not assigned when $\texttt{en}=0$; (ii) blocking assignments are illegal in every combinational $\texttt{always}$ block, so Z would be correct if it used $\texttt{<=}$; (iii) fragment X already swaps because both left-hand sides are updated. Do not treat any fragment as a two-flop CDC synchronizer, and do not introduce a second clock.

